(112,60,'mple, or\nthe prompt. In the following sections, we present the details\nof each category along with an overarching equation and the\ncorresponding methods. The adaptation component in each\nequation is highlighted in red.\n3.1 Model adaptation\nAn intuitive way of test-time adaptation is by adjusting the\nsource-trained model parametersθsaccording to the target\n\n4Target samples \nTest-time loss\nSource-trained model \nAdapted model \nPredictions\nBackward pass\nFeedforward pass \nFigure 3:Model adaptation.These methods gradually update their source-trained model by backpropagating a self-trained\nloss on target test data.\ndataxt. By doing so, obtaining the target-specific model\nparametersθt. Most model adaptation methods adjust their\nsource-trained model by fine-tuning the model parameters\nthrough unsupervised losses on the target test data, see\nFigure.\nGiven an unsupervised lossL(xt;θ) on target data, the\ntarget-specific modelθtand the predictionytis obtained by\nθ\n′\nt= min\nθ\nL(xt;θs) =θs+η∇θL(xt;θs),yt=fθ\n′\nt\n(xt),\n(2)\nwhere∇θL(xt;θs) denotes the parameter gradient of un-\nsupervised lossLandηdenotes the learning rate of\nbackpropagation. The adaptation ofθ\n′\ntrequires iterative\noptimization. We categorize the model adaptation methods\nfurther according to their unsupervised loss functions.\nAuxiliary self-supervision.Sunet al.[293] pioneered model\nadaptation by self-supervision. Their test-time training pro-\ncedure introduces an auxiliary self-supervised task with the\nloss functionL\na\n(x) . The model parametersθare split into the\nmain-task parametersθ\nmand the shared parametersθ\ne. The\nauxiliary task also has its own task-specific parametersθ\na.\nThe standard empirical risk minimizationminθLs(xs,ys;θ)\nduring source training is then changed to:\nmin\nθ\ne\n,θ\nm\n,θ\na\nL\nm\n(xs,ys;θ\ne\n,θ\nm\n) +L\na\n(xs;θ\ne\n,θ\na\n),(3)\nwhereL\nmandL\nadenote the loss function of the main task\nand auxiliary task, respectively. Aft