(113,60,'\ne\ns,θ\nm\ns,θ\na\ns)	. At test time, the main-\ntask modelθ\nmis fixed while the shared modelθ\neis fine-\ntuned by minimizing the auxiliary task loss on the target\ndataxt, which is formulated as:\nθ\ne\nt= min\nθe\nL\na\n(xt;θ\ne\ns,θ\na\ns). (4)\nFinally, the model makes predictions on the target data using\nthe target modelθ\n′\nt= (θ\ne\nt,θ\nm\ns) . Since the model is optimized\nonly by the auxiliary task during training, it is necessary\nto avoid model overfitting on the auxiliary task. Therefore,\nthe training stage is altered to jointly optimize the model by\nthe main task and the auxiliary task, expecting to make the\nadaptation of the auxiliary task compatible with the main\ntask [202], [293].\nTo further enhance model adaptation by auxiliary self-\nsupervision, several auxiliary tasks have been proposed.\nVarsavskyet al.[311] design an adversarial loss and augmen-\ntation consistency regularization. TTT++ [202] introduces\nSimCLR [37] as the self-supervised auxiliary task. They also\npropose an online feature alignment strategy, which aligns\nthe feature distributions at test time with the one at training\ntime to avoid overfitting to the auxiliary task. TTT-MAE\n[81] adapts the model for each test input using masked\nautoencoders [105] for self-supervision. Mate [220] utilizes a\n3D self-supervised reconstruction loss for model adaptation\nin a 3D classification setting. Diffusion-TTA [254] utilizes the\nfeedback of generative models to adapt the discriminative\nmodel. NC-TTT [240] utilizes the discrimination of noisy\nfeatures for adaptation. MT3 [19] relies on BYOL [96] as the\nauxiliary task for test-time training. Both Sainet al.[267] and\nLiuet al.[191] supervise their model adaptation procedure\nby a self-supervised image reconstruction objective. ClusT3\n[99] introduces an unsupervised loss, maximizing the mutual\ninformation between the features of different layers.\nBesides innovations on the self-supervision, many meth-\nods enforc