(114,60,'e feature alignment strategy, which aligns\nthe feature distributions at test time with the one at training\ntime to avoid overfitting to the auxiliary task. TTT-MAE\n[81] adapts the model for each test input using masked\nautoencoders [105] for self-supervision. Mate [220] utilizes a\n3D self-supervised reconstruction loss for model adaptation\nin a 3D classification setting. Diffusion-TTA [254] utilizes the\nfeedback of generative models to adapt the discriminative\nmodel. NC-TTT [240] utilizes the discrimination of noisy\nfeatures for adaptation. MT3 [19] relies on BYOL [96] as the\nauxiliary task for test-time training. Both Sainet al.[267] and\nLiuet al.[191] supervise their model adaptation procedure\nby a self-supervised image reconstruction objective. ClusT3\n[99] introduces an unsupervised loss, maximizing the mutual\ninformation between the features of different layers.\nBesides innovations on the self-supervision, many meth-\nods enforce better compatibility and cooperation between\nthe main and auxiliary tasks. Liet al.[180] introduce a trans-\nformer to better couple the relationship between the main\nand auxiliary tasks for human pose estimation. ActMAD\n[222] proposes activation matching for more fine-grained\nsupervision. OWTTT [181] extends model adaptation by self-\nsupervision on open-world data, supervised by clustering\nand distribution alignment at test time. Several methods [4],\n[36], [43], [218] learn to enhance the cooperation between\nmain and auxiliary tasks through meta-learning [74], [75],\n[307], [336]. Tailoring [4] trains its model to perform well\non the task loss after adaptation using the unsupervised\nloss. MT3 [19] uses the same approach to achieve model\nadaptation on a single unlabeled image. Minet al.[218]\ndeploy the meta-learning strategy into optical flow networks.\nModel adaptation by auxiliary self-supervision requires\ncareful design of the auxiliary tasks and their coupling with\nthe main task to avoid overfit