(115,60,'and auxiliary tasks. Liet al.[180] introduce a trans-\nformer to better couple the relationship between the main\nand auxiliary tasks for human pose estimation. ActMAD\n[222] proposes activation matching for more fine-grained\nsupervision. OWTTT [181] extends model adaptation by self-\nsupervision on open-world data, supervised by clustering\nand distribution alignment at test time. Several methods [4],\n[36], [43], [218] learn to enhance the cooperation between\nmain and auxiliary tasks through meta-learning [74], [75],\n[307], [336]. Tailoring [4] trains its model to perform well\non the task loss after adaptation using the unsupervised\nloss. MT3 [19] uses the same approach to achieve model\nadaptation on a single unlabeled image. Minet al.[218]\ndeploy the meta-learning strategy into optical flow networks.\nModel adaptation by auxiliary self-supervision requires\ncareful design of the auxiliary tasks and their coupling with\nthe main task to avoid overfitting. Often necessitating both\nmain and auxiliary losses, leading to an alternating training\nstage with extra computational costs. As an alternative,\nentropy minimization of model predictions is proposed to\nachieve test-time model adaptation without any auxiliary\ntask.\nEntropy minimization.Model adaptation by entropy min-\n\n5\nimization does not introduce any change in the source-\ntraining procedure, but only adapts the source-trained model\nto the target data at test time. Wanget al.[319] propose Tent,\nwhich introduces the concept of fully test-time adaptation.\nIt fine-tunes the source-trained model parameters on target\ndistributions directly by minimizing the entropy of the model\npredictions.\nθ\n′\nt= min\nθ\nL(xt,θs) = min\nθ\nH(ˆyt), (5)\nwhere the entropyH(ˆyt) is conducted on the source-model\npredictions on the target sampleˆyt=fθs\n(xt) . Different\nfrom auxiliary self-supervised methods that split the main\ntask parametersθinto shared parametersθeand specific\nparametersθmand only 