(130,60,'a-learning\n[294], [341].\nDiscussion.Compared to model adaptation, inference adap-\ntation methods are more efficient since they achieve model\ngeneration and model updates by a single feedforward pass\nof the target data, without backpropagation. However, most\ninference adaptation methods require additional designs of\nthe source-training stage, which may be not as convenient\nas model adaptation methods. Moreover, due to the large\nnumber of network model parameters, the model inference\nmethods often can only focus on a subset of the parameters,\ne.g., the classifier layer [63], [131], [341]. As different distri-\nbution shifts are influenced by different network layers [173],\nit is difficult for these methods to achieve good adaptation.\n3.3 Normalization adaptation\nSince adjusting model parameters can be computationally\nexpensive and training dependent, normalization adaptation\nmethods focus on adjusting only the normalization statistics\nof the widely used batch normalization layers [130]. batch\nnormalization is known to reduce the internal covariate shift\nby normalizing the input features of subsequent layers by\nˆxs=\nxs−µs\n√\nσ\n2\ns\n+ϵ\n, whereµsandσ\n2\nsare the expectation and\nvariance of the mini-batch of samples during source training.\nSince the target dataxtand source statisticsµs, σs are\nmismatched, the normalization adaptation methods estimate\nthe target statisticsµt, σt to normalize the target features\nwhile fixing the source-trained model parameters, as shown\nin Figure. The adaptation process is formulated as:\nµt,t=h(xt, µs, σs),ˆxt=\nxt−µt\np\nσ\n2\nt+ϵ\n,yt=fθs(ˆxt),(9)\nwhereh(·)denotes the target statistics estimation module.\nh(·)can be either parametric or non-parametric, and the\ninputs of the module are usually the target featuresxt,\nsometimes together with the source statisticsµs, σs.\nSome model adaptation methods from Section\nchange the batch normalization statistics to improve per-\nformance, e.g., Tent and 