(131,60,'tch\nnormalization is known to reduce the internal covariate shift\nby normalizing the input features of subsequent layers by\nˆxs=\nxs−µs\n√\nσ\n2\ns\n+ϵ\n, whereµsandσ\n2\nsare the expectation and\nvariance of the mini-batch of samples during source training.\nSince the target dataxtand source statisticsµs, σs are\nmismatched, the normalization adaptation methods estimate\nthe target statisticsµt, σt to normalize the target features\nwhile fixing the source-trained model parameters, as shown\nin Figure. The adaptation process is formulated as:\nµt,t=h(xt, µs, σs),ˆxt=\nxt−µt\np\nσ\n2\nt+ϵ\n,yt=fθs(ˆxt),(9)\nwhereh(·)denotes the target statistics estimation module.\nh(·)can be either parametric or non-parametric, and the\ninputs of the module are usually the target featuresxt,\nsometimes together with the source statisticsµs, σs.\nSome model adaptation methods from Section\nchange the batch normalization statistics to improve per-\nformance, e.g., Tent and some subsequent methods [286],\n[319], [349] estimate the target mini-batch statistics during\nfine-tuning the affine parameters of the batch normalization\nlayers. In this section, we focus on methods that only\nadjust the normalization statistics, without altering their\nmodel parameters. We divide current methods into three\ntypes according to the target statistics estimation method:\ncalculating target statistics directly, combining source and\ntarget statistics, and target statistics inference.\nTarget statistics.To make the target data fit source-trained\nmodel parameters, the target featuresˆxtat each layer of the\ndeep neural network need to be normalized at test time,\nsimilarly to the source features during training. One of\nthe most straightforward methods is to directly estimate\nthe statistics of the target data in the batch normalization\nlayers at test time [179], [228]. Nado	et al.[228] propose\nprediction-time batch normalization to recompute the Batch\nNorm statistics, i.e.,µtandσ\n2\nt