(134,60,'combination methods aggregate both source and\ntarget data statistics to balance source-learned knowledge\nand target-specific information. Statistics inference methods\ntrain a network to infer target-specific statistics.\nAdapted statistics used at test time Equation examples\nTarget statistics\nTarget batch statistics, e.g., [228], [376]\nµt=\n1\nM H W\nP\nm,h,w\nXt,\nσ\n2\nt\n=\n1\nM H W\nP\nm,h,w\n(Xt−µt)\n2\nTarget instance statistics, e.g., [90], [142]\nµt=\n1\nH W\nP\nh,w\nxt,\nσ\n2\nt\n=\n1\nH W\nP\nh,w\n(xt−µt)\n2\nStatistics combination\nBatch-wise combination, e.g., [271], [359]\n¯µ=αµt+ (1−α)µs,\n¯σ=ασt+ (1−α)σs\nMoving-average combination, e.g., [123], [221]\n¯µk+1= (1−λ)¯µk+λµt,\n¯σk+1= (1−λ)¯σk+λσt\nStatistics inference\nInferred statistics, e.g., [61], [138]	(µt, σt) =gψ(xt)\nReplacing the source statistics directly with the target\nones can alleviate the covariate shifts between source and\ntarget distributions with sufficient target samples. However,\nbatch normalization always requires large batch sizes or\nmoving averages of large numbers of samples for accurate\nestimation of the statistics. When the target samples are\ninsufficient or from various distributions, the estimated target\nstatistics will not be able to represent the target distribu-\ntion well, leading to unstable prediction and performance\ndegradation. UnMix-TNS [299] re-calibrates the statistics for\neach test sample by mixing it with multiple online statistics,\nwhich are online updated by the most similar samples.\nKakuet al.[142] replace the batch normalization with\ninstance normalization [305], which considers the instance-\nwise statistics and is widely adopted in estimating style\n[126], task-specific [26], or domain-specific [273] information.\nGonget al.[90] proposes instance-aware batch normalization,\nwhich corrects the training batch normalization statistics\nby the instance normalization statistics when the instance-\nwise statistics of the target sample ar