(136,60,'he training one. Instance normalization addresses the\nrequirement for large numbers of i.i.d target samples of batch\nnormalization, but it makes the features less discriminative\nover classes [273]. Therefore, it is unsuitable for complex\ntasks with a large number of categories. In addition, large\ndistribution shifts between the source and target domains\ncan result in a mismatch between target batch statistics and\nsource parameters, which will potentially deteriorate the\ndiscriminative structures and perturb the predictions [359].\nTherefore, methods that combine source and target statistics\nare proposed to estimate more stable normalization statistics\nfor domain shifts.\nStatistics combination.One of the most common methods\nfor combination is the weighted sum of the source and target\nstatistics [186], [271], [353]. Schneider	et al.[271] introduce a\nhyperparameterMto combine and trade off the source and\ntarget statistics when the number of available target samples\nnis too small. The combined statistics is obtained by¯µ=\nM\nM+m\nµs+\nm\nM+m\nµt\n,¯σ\n2\n=\nM\nM+m\nσ\n2\ns+\nm\nM+m\nσ\n2\nt\n. Similarly, You\net al.[359] proposeα-BN to calibrate the batch normalization\nstatistics as¯µ=αµt+(1−α)µs	,¯σ=ασt+(1−α)σs	, where\nαis also a hyperparameter. To extend the method to the case\nwith only a single target sample, SITA [148] obtains	µtandσt\nof the aforementioned equations from various augmentations\nof the target sample. They achieve a hyperparameter-free\ncombination algorithm by selecting prior hyperparameter\nvalues using the prediction entropy. TEMA [288] proposes\nan exponential moving average method to combine training\nand test statistics. TTN [186] converts the hyperparameter to\nthe learnable parameter. The parameterαis initialized by a\ngradient distance score and optimized during the proposed\npost-training phase. At test time, the batch normalization\nstatistics are obtained by¯µ=αµt+ (1−α)µs	,¯σ\n2\n=ασ\n2\nt+\n(1−α)σ\n2\ns+α(1−α