(141,60,'lthough there are various ways to\nalleviate this problem, e.g., instance normalization statistics,\ncombination with source statistics, and statistics inference,\nthey all have their own disadvantages. The instance normal-\nization statistics can result in less discrimination of the model.\nThe combination of source and target statistics requires care-\nful selection or training of the hyperparameters and update\nprocess to trade off the source and target information. The\nstatistics are difficult to estimate by a single sample, which\nrequires complex methods and various source distributions\nto mimic the adaptation procedure during training.\n3.4 Sample adaptation\nAs aforementioned, adapting model parameters can be\ncomputationally expensive and unstable, especially for large\nmodels, and normalization adaptation methods are limited to\nspecific architectures [106]. To solve these problems, sample\nadaptation methods are proposed to maintain the knowledge\nin the pretrained models at test time and instead adjust the\ntarget samples. As illustrated in Figure, sample adaptation\nmethods adapt the target dataxtthrough moduleψ, and\nmake predictions on the adapted datax\n′\nsby the fixed source-\ntrained modelθs:\nx\n′\ns=ψ(xt),yt=fθs\n(x\n′\ns). (10)\nwhereψdenotes the adaptation module.\nSample adaptation methods [83], [233], [243], [340] usu-\nally adapt their target data to the source data distributions\nthrough generative models, e.g., generative adversarial\nnetworks [92], variational auto-encoder [153], energy-based\nmodels [60], [112], and diffusion models [113], [282]. The\ngeneration-based methods are inspired by human visual\nrecognition, where the challenging images are related to\nknown familiar ones through iterative feedback for improved\nrecognition [127], [243]. Based on the generative models,\nthese methods generate the corresponding source samples or\nfeatures conditioned on the target ones and make predictions\non the adapted data. In th