(142,60,'nstead adjust the\ntarget samples. As illustrated in Figure, sample adaptation\nmethods adapt the target dataxtthrough moduleψ, and\nmake predictions on the adapted datax\n′\nsby the fixed source-\ntrained modelθs:\nx\n′\ns=ψ(xt),yt=fθs\n(x\n′\ns). (10)\nwhereψdenotes the adaptation module.\nSample adaptation methods [83], [233], [243], [340] usu-\nally adapt their target data to the source data distributions\nthrough generative models, e.g., generative adversarial\nnetworks [92], variational auto-encoder [153], energy-based\nmodels [60], [112], and diffusion models [113], [282]. The\ngeneration-based methods are inspired by human visual\nrecognition, where the challenging images are related to\nknown familiar ones through iterative feedback for improved\nrecognition [127], [243]. Based on the generative models,\nthese methods generate the corresponding source samples or\nfeatures conditioned on the target ones and make predictions\non the adapted data. In these methods, the adaptation\nmoduleψin eq.\nwhich is learned to model the source data distributions\nand adapt data from unseen distributions to the source\ndistributions. Since the adaptation of the target samples can\nbe conducted in both feature or input space, we categorize\nrecent sample adaptation approaches into feature adjustment\nand input adjustment methods.\nFeature adjustment.Since features are learned to extract\nuseful information from the input while eliminating redun-\ndant details, modeling and adjusting information in the\nfeature space are efficient and easy to accomplish. Thus, most\nsample adaptation methods are applied in the feature space.\nHuanget al.[127] utilize recurrent feedback for the out-of-\ndistribution images. Their method adds generative feedback\nconnections with latent variables to the neural networks.\nThe generative feedback updates the features of the target\nsamples with the predictions and latent variables iteratively.\nPandeyet al.[243] first learn a domain inv