(149,60,'tilizes large language models to\niteratively update the prompt for each test sample for the\ntext-to-image generation task. Beyond adapting prompts in\nthe text space, there are also more general prompt adaptation\nmethods conducted in the embedding space.\nEmbedding-prompting.These methods leverage the ad-\nvancements of the transformer architecture [312], where\nthe prompts are fed into the network together with the\ntarget input data to adjust the prediction function. Some\nprompt adaptation methods [86], [290] are based on the\nVision Transformer [135]. Gao	et al.[86] tune the visual\nprompts by a hierarchical self-supervised loss function.\nCVP [302] designs convolutional visual prompts to use the\nconvolutional structure as an inductive bias for visual test-\ntime adaptation tasks. U-VPA [80] introduces an uncertainty-\nguided updating strategy for adaptation to continually\nchanging environments. Ganet al.[79] leverage visual\nprompt adaptation into continual test-time adaptation to\navoid catastrophic forgetting. TPGaze [192] utilizes prompts\nto replace the original zero padding of the input images. Niu\net al.[236] propose forward-only prompt adaptation by a\nderivative-free optimizer, covariance matrix adaptation, to\nadjust prompts at test time. Heet al.[104] introduce domain\nshift tokens at transformer layers and learn them by bilevel\noptimization in an adversarial manner.\nThere are also methods adapting prompts in the textual\nembedding space for multimodal models [269], [279], [338],\n[370], [374]. TPT [279] proposes prompt tuning for the\nmultimodal CLIP model [258]. To improve the effectiveness\nof prompt adaptation with limited data, DiffTPT [73] utilizes\npretrained diffusion models to generate diverse data for\nadaptation. SwapPrompt [211] leverages self-supervised\ncontrastive learning to facilitate prompt adaptation. Samadh\net al.[269] align the test sample statistics to the offline\nsource statistics at test time. C-TPT [358] exp