(150,60,'rophic forgetting. TPGaze [192] utilizes prompts\nto replace the original zero padding of the input images. Niu\net al.[236] propose forward-only prompt adaptation by a\nderivative-free optimizer, covariance matrix adaptation, to\nadjust prompts at test time. Heet al.[104] introduce domain\nshift tokens at transformer layers and learn them by bilevel\noptimization in an adversarial manner.\nThere are also methods adapting prompts in the textual\nembedding space for multimodal models [269], [279], [338],\n[370], [374]. TPT [279] proposes prompt tuning for the\nmultimodal CLIP model [258]. To improve the effectiveness\nof prompt adaptation with limited data, DiffTPT [73] utilizes\npretrained diffusion models to generate diverse data for\nadaptation. SwapPrompt [211] leverages self-supervised\ncontrastive learning to facilitate prompt adaptation. Samadh\net al.[269] align the test sample statistics to the offline\nsource statistics at test time. C-TPT [358] explores calibration\nduring prompt adaptation and considers the calibration\nerror for optimizing the prompts. DART [203] introduces\nboth class-specific text prompts and instance-level image\nprompts, which are adaptively updated on subsequent test\nsamples. RLCF [385] further adopts the CLIP model as a\nreward model to provide feedback during test-time prompt\ntuning. Unlike the optimization-based methods, any-shift\nprompting [338] generates the test-specific prompt in a\nfeedforward pass, without fine-tuning at test time. Zanella\net al.[368] introduce test-time augmentation techniques to\nreplace test-time prompt tuning for the CLIP model. ZERO\n[71] improves test-time prompt tuning methods by simply\naggregating predictions on selected augmentations with zero\nsoftmax temperature.\nDiscussion.Most prompt adaptation methods in the embed-\nding space follow a model adaptation strategy by fine-tuning\nthe prompts during test time. Unlike model adaptation,\nprompt adaptation does not require adjusting th