(155,60,'ks.\nMost inference adaptation methods also need extra\npreparation during source training since they require training\nextra modules for the inference of model parameters at test\ntime [63], [151], [326], [355]. Although only estimating nor-\nmalization statistics, the normalization adaptation methods\nInstCal-U [401] and TTN [186] also require preparation since\nthe method learns the parameters for combining source and\ntarget statistics in a post-training stage.\nSample adaptation methods [83], [127], [233], [243] usu-\nally require preparations in training to model the source\ndistribution. In addition to the neural network for the main\ntask, these methods also need to train the generation model\nto encode the source distribution and learn to update the\ntarget data to the source at test time.\nTraining-and-data preparation.In addition to altering train-\ning strategies, some methods [4], [19], [61], [294], [341] further\nchange the training data to simulate distribution shifts during\ntraining to achieve more robust and generalizable test-time\nadaptation. These methods require preparations for both the\ntraining strategy and the data in the source-training stage.\nIn model adaptation, training-and-data preparation is\ncommon for meta-learning-based methods to strengthen the\nrelationship between the auxiliary tasks and the primary task\n[4], [19], [36], [40], [42], [43], [103], [128], [191], [193], [218],\n[267], [292], [336], [388]. These methods follow the algorithm\nof model agnostic meta-learning [74], designing an inner\nloop to update the model of the auxiliary tasks and an outer\nloop to minimize the objectives of the primary task for the\nupdated model parameters with specific data splits. By doing\nso, the model learns good initialization parameters that can\nquickly adapt to unseen distributions with the auxiliary task\n[128], [267]. In other words, the model learns the underlying\nrelationship between the primary and auxiliary tasks, which