(160,60,'ture adjustment - [127], [233], [243] [248], []\nInput adjustment - [83], [239], [301] -\nPrompt adaptation\nText-prompting [66], [261], [262], [378] - -\nEmbedding-prompting [86], [211], [279], [358], [385] - [338]\nreal-world applications. In this section, we categorize the\nexisting methods according to their update strategies and\ninference data.\n5.1 Update strategies\nAccording to their update strategy, we divide existing\ntest-time adaptation methods into two categories, iterative\nupdate and on-the-fly update, which are highly related to\ncomputational costs and adaptation efficiency. The iterative\nupdate methods usually require iterative optimizations of the\nmodel parameters or target samples to achieve adaptation,\ne.g., [83], [293], whereas the on-the-fly update methods\nachieve adaptation in only a single pass of the data, e.g.,\n[61], [341].\nIterative update.Most model adaptation methods [34], [95],\n[134], [238], [293], [319], [324], [375] are iterative at inference\nsince they require step-by-step fine-tuning to update their\nmodel parameters at test time [81], [173], [202], [279], [293],\n[319]. Since fine-tuning usually comes with high computa-\ntional costs, some model adaptation methods propose fine-\ntuning only a subset of the model parameters at test time\nto improve their adaptation efficiency and stability. Tent\n[319] only updates the statistics and affine parameters of the\nbatch normalization layers, which is followed by many [93],\n[281], [366], [375]. Others introduce additional lightweight\nadaptive models and only update the adaptive parameters\nfor efficient inference [35], [284]. Recent works find that\ndifferent models [182] or fine-tuning different blocks [173]\nperforms best for different distribution shifts. Therefore,\nLeeet al.[173] propose to selectively adapt a subset of the\nmodel parameters according to the distribution shifts. Tang\net al.[296] propose to learn lower layer representations by a\nfeedforward Hebb