(162,60,'eters partially, these methods achieve a good trade-off\nbetween computational costs and adaptation performance.\nLike model adaptation, most sample adaptation methods\n[83], [127], [243], [340] also require iterative updates by\nupdating the target samples to the source distributions step-\nby-step through generative models like energy-based model\nand diffusion model. Many prompt adaptation methods also\nfollow the adaptation strategy of model adaptation, therefore\nalso requiring iterative inference at test time [66], [86], [211],\n[213], [269], [279], [290], [358], [378], [385]. Although avoiding\nfine-tuning the model parameters, they adapt the prompts\nby iteratively updating them by backpropagation [211], [269],\n[279] or large language model feedback [213], [378], [385] at\ntest time.\nOn-the-fly update.Different from the iterative methods, on-\nthe-fly update achieves adaptation in a single feedforward\npass at test time, without the need for fine-tuning or\nbackpropagation. All inference adaptation methods (e.g.,\n[63], [124], [131], [151], [294], [341], [355], [379]) perform\nan on-the-fly update since these methods directly infer the\nmodel parameters at test time. The normalization adaptation\nmethods [61], [90], [186], [228], [271] are also on the fly, by\nestimating their normalization statistics in a single feedfor-\nward pass. vAs a sample adaptation method, test-time style\nshifting [248] achieves an on-the-fly update by adapting the\ntarget features through the distribution statistics, without\niterative update. Some prompt adaptation methods can also\nbe on-the-fly at inference by utilizing pretrained large models\n[66], [219], [261], [262] or by learning the ability of prompt\ngeneration during training [338].\nDiscussions.Given enough time and computational re-\nsources, iterative update methods can yield reliable adap-\ntation results. However, they may struggle in real-world\napplications that have strict computational cost requireme