(163,60,'ation methods (e.g.,\n[63], [124], [131], [151], [294], [341], [355], [379]) perform\nan on-the-fly update since these methods directly infer the\nmodel parameters at test time. The normalization adaptation\nmethods [61], [90], [186], [228], [271] are also on the fly, by\nestimating their normalization statistics in a single feedfor-\nward pass. vAs a sample adaptation method, test-time style\nshifting [248] achieves an on-the-fly update by adapting the\ntarget features through the distribution statistics, without\niterative update. Some prompt adaptation methods can also\nbe on-the-fly at inference by utilizing pretrained large models\n[66], [219], [261], [262] or by learning the ability of prompt\ngeneration during training [338].\nDiscussions.Given enough time and computational re-\nsources, iterative update methods can yield reliable adap-\ntation results. However, they may struggle in real-world\napplications that have strict computational cost requirements.\nOn-the-fly update is more time-efficient for adaptation and\nprediction during test time, making them better suited for\nreal-time applications. Nevertheless, these methods can be\nconstrained by complex training strategies [61], [341], [355]\nor specific applications [131], [186], [271].\n5.2 Inference data\nBesides the update strategy, the accessible target data is also\nan essential factor for test time adaptation. In real-world\napplications, target distributions are typically unknown,\nmaking it challenging to collect sufficient data from specific\ntarget distributions. Therefore, the inference data is crucial for\nthe deployment of test-time adaptation approaches. Based on\n\n13\nthe data requirements of the target distribution, we classify\ncurrent test-time adaptation methods into four categories:\nonline, batch-wise, sample-wise, and dynamic inference.\nOnline inference.With the goal of achieving adaptation\nalong with inference, online inference is a popular strategy\nin test-time adapta