(166,60,'hese methods struggle with complex settings,\nsuch as having limited target data and intricate target distri-\nbutions, where the methods may lead to error accumulation\nor knowledge forgetting.\nBatch-wise inference.Batch-wise inference methods achieve\nadaptation for each batch of target samples to avoid error\naccumulation among different batches. Normalization adap-\ntation methods [186], [228], [271], [359], [376] usually focus\non batch-wise inference since target batch statistics are impor-\ntant for the statistics estimation. Some inference adaptation\nmethods [63], [355] also utilize the information within each\ntarget batch to infer target-specific model parameters. Batch-\nwise inference reduces error accumulation in online settings.\nHowever, it still requires each batch of samples to originate\nfrom the same target distribution, which may not be true in\nreal-world applications.\nSample-wise inference.To further reduce the requirement of\ntarget sample amounts and fit complex target distributions,\nsample-wise inference methods are proposed to achieve\nadaptation for each individual target sample [45], [55], [83],\n[107], [118], [137], [279], [381]. By adapting each target\nsample, the methods can handle test data from various target\ndistributions without error accumulation and forgetting.\nThrough updating each target sample individually, all sample\nadaptation methods [83], [127], [243], [248], [340] belong to\nthe sample-wise regime. Some model adaptation methods\nachieve sample-wise inference by fine-tuning their model\nparameters on the augmentations of each target sample [293],\n[375]. SiSTA [297] fine-tunes a generative model using a\nsingle-shot target, and samples the synthetic target data\nfor adaptation at test time. ViTTA [188] deploys temporal\naugmentation for sample-wise test-time adaptation on video\ndata. Meta-learning is also utilized in model adaptation\nmethods to learn the ability of sample-wise inference during\ntraining 