(175,60,'d in test-time adap-\ntation is the corruption [110], e.g., CIFAR-10-C, CIFAR-100-Noise Blur Weather Digital\nPhoto Art-painting Cartoon Sketch\nCorruptions  \nImage styles \nFigure 8:Examples of covariate shifts in image classification.\nCorruptions (first row) are usually evaluated for single-\nsource settings, while image styles are usually evaluated\nin multi-source settings, following the common experimental\nsetup in domain generalization.\nC, and ImageNet-C [83], [186], [271], [293], [319], [375]. A\nmodel is trained on the original dataset, e.g., CIFAR [158]\nor ImageNet [265]. The target distributions are obtained by\napplying 15 types of corruption at five severity levels on the\noriginal test set of the datasets, some samples are shown in\nFigure.\nDigit adaptation is a common benchmark in domain adap-\ntation, where different distributions are different datasets,\ne.g., MNIST [163], MNIST-M [82], SVHN [230], and USPS\n[129]. The distributions have the same label space with\n10 categories. Following [319], most test-time adaptation\nmethods on digits datasets train their model on SVHN. The\nsource-trained model is then adapted and evaluated on the\ndifferent target distributions of MNIST, MNIST-M, and USPS.\nThere are also other test-time adaptation datasets based\non common image classification benchmarks like CIFAR\nand ImageNet.CIFAR-10.1[259] is a test set for CIFAR-10\nthat consists of images not present in the original CIFAR-10\ndataset. While both datasets share the same 10 classes, the\n\n15\nTable 4:Evaluating test-time distribution shifts.This table summarizes representative test-time adaptation methods\naccording to what they adapt (Section) and the distribution shift they address in their image classification evaluation\n(Section), revealing the emerging research opportunity of test-time adaptation beyond model adaptation and covariate\nshifts (Section).\nCovariate shifts\nLabel shifts Conditional shifts Joint shifts\nSingle-source Mu