(176,60,'wing [319], most test-time adaptation\nmethods on digits datasets train their model on SVHN. The\nsource-trained model is then adapted and evaluated on the\ndifferent target distributions of MNIST, MNIST-M, and USPS.\nThere are also other test-time adaptation datasets based\non common image classification benchmarks like CIFAR\nand ImageNet.CIFAR-10.1[259] is a test set for CIFAR-10\nthat consists of images not present in the original CIFAR-10\ndataset. While both datasets share the same 10 classes, the\n\n15\nTable 4:Evaluating test-time distribution shifts.This table summarizes representative test-time adaptation methods\naccording to what they adapt (Section) and the distribution shift they address in their image classification evaluation\n(Section), revealing the emerging research opportunity of test-time adaptation beyond model adaptation and covariate\nshifts (Section).\nCovariate shifts\nLabel shifts Conditional shifts Joint shifts\nSingle-source Multi-source\nModel adaptation\nAuxiliary self-supervision [19], [81], [202], [293] [35] - - -\nEntropy minimization [44], [319], [375] [44], [372] [173], [249], [398] [87], [170], [173], [249]\nPseudo-labeling [34], [95], [298] [7], [134], [325] - - -\nFeature alignment [76], [141], [231], [285] [325] - - -\nInference adaptation\nBatch-wise inference [124], [379] [63], [131], [379] - - -\nSample-wise inference - [294], [341] - - -\nNormalization adaptation\nTarget statistics [90], [228], [376] - - - -\nStatistics combination [186], [271] - - - -\nStatistics inference - [61], [138] - - -\nSample adaptation\nFeature adjustment - [243], [248], [340] - - -\nInput adjustment [83] - - - -\nPrompt adaptation\nText-prompting [119] - [119], [214], [217] - [119]\nEmbedding-prompting [86], [269], [279], [338] [338] [338] [338] [338]\nimages in CIFAR-10.1 are selected to be more challenging and\nless similar to the CIFAR-10 training set, which is referred\nto as the natural distributional shift.ImageNet-A[111] also\nfo