(194,60,'mpt adaptation\nmethods. These areas are worth exploring in future research.\nIn contrast, sample adaptation is more challenging to apply\nin video applications due to the complexity of video data\nand the difficulty in spatio-temporal generative modeling of\nthe source distribution.\n7.3 3D-level applications\nBeyond the image and video applications, 3D-level applica-\ntions also face distribution shifts, which require adaptation at\ntest time. Test-time adaptation for 3D applications currently\nfocuses on tasks like classification and segmentation.\n3D classification.3D classification tasks make predictions\non a 3D point cloud, which can be corrupted at test time.\nThe test-time adaptation methods for 3D classification tries\nto solve distribution shifts in 3D-level through either model\nadaptation [220], [277] or inference adaptation [327].\n3D dense prediction.In addition to classification, 3D dense\nsegmentation [29], [30], [253], [268], [278], [332] and detection\n[187], [364] are popular applications for test-time adaptation.\nMethods for 3D segmentation, like those for 2D dense pre-\ndiction tasks, primarily rely on model adaptation techniques.\nThese include approaches using 3D reconstruction [253]\nor pseudo-labeling [268], [400] at test time. Some methods\n\n18\nTable 5:Test-time adaptation applications.This table summarizes representative test-time adaptation methods according to\nwhat they adapt (Section) and their main applications (Section) revealing the emerging research opportunity of test-time\nadaptation beyond model adaptation and image classification (Section).\nImage-level Video-level 3D-level Beyond-vision\nClassification Dense Enhance Classification Dense Enhance Classification Dense RL NLP Multimodal\nModel adaptation\nAuxiliary self-supervision [202], [293] [246] [40], [263] - [22], [199] [41], [351] [220] [253], [332] [197], [283] [16], [102]\nEntropy minimization [319], [375] [170], [281] - [356] - - - - - - [196], [334]\nPseudo